 ],
 "literal": "In the small village of few households with house-brewed toddy, / having offered the morning sacrifice to the hero-stone planted on the side, / having bathed it with good water and kindled ghee-perfumed incense, / the dark smoke like a cloud spreads fragrant along with the street. / Even though it is a settlement on a difficult frontier, like an anthill where a striped-necked / snake dwells, it is indeed so. Every day, / without looking at the distress of other patrons, to the suppliants, / without diminishing, giving generosity, / the praise-worthy great hero protects this village!",
 "translation": "In this small village of a few households where toddy is brewed at home, they bathe the hero-stone planted on the outskirts with pure water, offer the morning sacrifice, and burn fragrant incense with ghee. The dark smoke, like a cloud, spreads its fragrance throughout the street. Although it is a settlement on a dangerous frontier, it is like an anthill inhabited by a striped-necked cobra. Here, every day, without seeking help from other patrons, the famous and noble chief protects his village, giving generously to suppliants without ever holding back.",
 "modern": "வீடுகளில் கள் காய்ச்சும் சில குடும்பங்களே வாழும் இந்தச் சிறிய ஊரின் எல்லையில் நடப்பட்டுள்ள நடுகல்லுக்கு, தூய நீரால் நீராட்டி, காலையிலேயே பலி கொடுத்து, நெய் கலந்த நறுமணப் புகையை ஏற்றி வழிபடுகின்றனர். மேகம் போன்ற அந்த அடர்ந்த நறுமணப் புகை தெரு முழுவதும் கமழ்கிறது. இது ஆபத்தான எல்லையில் உள்ள ஊராக இருந்தாலும், நஞ்சுடைய வரிப் பிடரிப் பாம்பு வாழும் புற்று போன்ற வலிமை உடையது. பிற வள்ளல்களின் உதவியை நாடாமல், இரப்பவர்க்கு எப்போதும் குறையாமல் வாரி வழங்கும் கொடைத்தன்மை கொண்ட, புகழ்பெற்ற பெருந்தகை காக்கும் ஊர் இதுவாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai and the theme of Mūtiṉ mullai, which praises the ancient martial lineage and valor of a warrior's home. The poet describes a small, fierce frontier village protected by a noble chieftain. The villagers worship their fallen heroes by bathing the hero-stones (naṭukal) and burning rich incense, creating a fragrant smoke that fills the streets. The poet compares the dangerous, heavily guarded village to an anthill inhabited by a deadly, striped-necked cobra, warning enemies to stay away. Despite its small size and constant state of war, the chieftain is incredibly generous, giving freely to all suppliants without needing to rely on wealthier patrons.",
